1 Introduction Ridge regression and early-stopped gradient descent are two of the most common regularization tools in high-dimensional linear regression. Ridge gives an explicit spectral shrinkage rule, while early stopping regularizes through the time profile of the optimization path (Hoerl and Kennard, 1970 ; Yao et al. , 2007 ; Dobriban and Wager, 2018 ; Ali et al. , 2019 ; Tsigler and Bartlett, 2023 ) . In overparameterized and anisotropic problems, however, the usual shrinkage picture is incomplete. A large collection of weak spectral directions can stabilize interpolation, but it can also make the signal-bearing part of the spectrum behave as if it were already ridge-penalized (Bartlett et al. , 2020 ; Hastie et al. , 2022 ; Tsigler and Bartlett, 2023 ) . This implicit shrinkage can be strong enough that the best ridge correction is negative. In the head–tail view of benign-overfitting ridge theory, many weak tail directions create an effective positive floor on the head coordinates; canceling this floor can require a negative ridge parameter (Tsigler and Bartlett, 2023 ) . Negative ridge is therefore a natural response to over-shrinkage: it pushes spectral filters above one on directions where the ridgeless interpolator under-estimates the signal. For a signed level ν ≥ 0 
